{"path": "poker.py", "content": "from collections import Counter

RANKS = {str(n): n for n in range(2, 11)}
RANKS.update({"J": 11, "Q": 12, "K": 13, "A": 14})


def _score(hand):
    cards = hand.split()
    values = [RANKS[card[:-1]] for card in cards]
    suits = [card[-1] for card in cards]

    counts = Counter(values)
    # Group by (count, value), highest first, e.g. full house -> [(3, v), (2, w)]
    groups = sorted(((c, v) for v, c in counts.items()), reverse=True)
    shape = [c for c, _ in groups]
    ordered = [v for _, v in groups]

    flush = len(set(suits)) == 1
    unique = sorted(counts, reverse=True)
    straight = False
    if len(unique) == 5:
        if unique[0] - unique[4] == 4:
            straight = True
        elif unique == [14, 5, 4, 3, 2]:
            straight = True
            ordered = [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]

    if straight and flush:
        category = 8
    elif shape == [4, 1]:
        category = 7
    elif shape == [3, 2]:
        category = 6
    elif flush:
        category = 5
    elif straight:
        category = 4
    elif shape == [3, 1, 1]:
        category = 3
    elif shape == [2, 2, 1]:
        category = 2
    elif shape == [2, 1, 1, 1]:
        category = 1
    else:
        category = 0
    return (category, ordered)


def best_hands(hands):
    scores = [_score(hand) for hand in hands]
    best = max(scores)
    return [hand for hand, score in zip(hands, scores) if score == best]
"}

{"summary": "`best_hands` in `poker.py` is now implemented with only the standard library. It returns every hand tied for best, in the order they were given.

Each hand gets a score made of its category (high card up to straight flush) and then its card values, sorted by group size first. That handles tie-breaks such as a full house's three cards counting before its pair, and kickers. Aces count high, except in A-2-3-4-5, which counts as a 5-high straight.

The project has no test files, so I checked it with inline asserts run through `python -c`. They cover the main exercise-style cases: one hand, high card, ties, two pair and full house tie-breaks, straights with a low ace, straight flushes, and a straight beating three of a kind. My first run failed because one of my own expected answers was wrong (three of a kind beat the hands I'd expected to win), not because of the code. After I corrected it, all the asserts passed.", "outputs": ["poker.py"]}
